{"records":[{"id":"vhzzzc6m36","title":"Diagnostic Discrimination of Zika and Dengue-2 Infection by High-Throughput TCR Sequencing","description":"The Zika virus has been listed as a global health threat by the World Health Organization due to its association with congenital birth defects. Current FDA-approved diagnostics are unable to distinguish between infection with Zika and other flaviviruses. Here, we report on the computational identification of virus-associated TCRβ-sequencing using high-throughput sequencing prior to and after infection with Zika or Dengue-2 viruses.  Computationally identified T cell receptor sequences were shown to be virus-specific and used to develop a diagnostic assay that correctly diagnoses Zika and Dengue-2 infected in mice with 97% accuracy. These results identify populations of TCR sequences specific for Zika and Dengue infection that can be used for diagnostic purposes, and serve as an important proof-of-principle, providing a novel platform for accurately diagnosing Zika virus infection. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-09-25T17:03:23.996Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vhzzzc6m36","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vhzzzc6m36"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kyle","last_name":"Wolf","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gfcn2wmrpf","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled \"A genetic map of the response to DNA damage in human cells\" by Olivieri M et al. Cell 2020","description":"We used pooled CRISPR/Cas9 screens in the human RPE1-hTERT p53-/- cell line against 27 genotoxic agents. The Dataset herein make the primary data available. The files are:\n\n* Additional genes of interest (discussion and data relating to TMEM2, ESD, USP37, PHF12, BTAF1 and DRAP1)\n* Supplementary Raw Data: Data used to make all the graphs in the manuscript\n* Folder \"CRISPR screen readcount files\". Readcounts for the screens undertaken as part of this study.\n* Folder \"Raw images for immunoblots\": uncropped images for all the blots in the manuscript\n* Folder: \"Additional QC analyses of CRISPR screens\": Additional analyses that measure the quality of the screens using Presion-Recall curves of essential genes and an estimate of genes that scored as hits due to batch effects.\n* Folder \"Data_Analysis_Code\" R markdown file along with data files\n* File: \"Additional Genes of Interests\" contains a short discussion of genes we found noteworthy after analysis of our screen data.\n\nVersion 2 note: this version differs from Version 1 by a single file (FDRPos_31screens.csv) which has a tab error fixed.\n\n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-07T14:26:40.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gfcn2wmrpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gfcn2wmrpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Olivieri","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Quality","Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yzbfm4v7y7","title":"Identification of novel antiviral drug candidates using an optimized SARS-CoV-2 phenotypic screening platform. Bojkova et al","description":"This table presents the results from a screen of kinase inhibitors for activity against a SARS-CoV-2 Delta isolate. Caco-2-F03 cells were infected with an MOI of 0.01 and virus infection was monitored using caspase 3/7 activity as read-out.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-04T09:41:42.535Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yzbfm4v7y7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yzbfm4v7y7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Michaelis","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmacology","Virology","Drug Screening"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x4msbrdgth","title":"Sex-specific effect of antenatal Zika virus infection on murine fetal growth, placental nutrient transporters, and nutrient sensor signaling pathways 2","description":"The present data supports the findings of the study titled ´Sex-specific effect of antenatal Zika virus infection on murine fetal growth, placental nutrient transporters, and nutrient sensor signaling pathways´.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-05-31T08:16:18.806Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x4msbrdgth","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x4msbrdgth"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniela","last_name":"Pereira-Carvalho","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Placenta","Zika Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s3xw35xczs","title":"Disrupting the HDAC6-ubiquitin interaction impairs infection by influenza and Zika virus and cellular stress pathways","description":"The original data for manuscript: \"Disrupting the HDAC6-ubiquitin interaction impairs infection by influenza and Zika virus and cellular stress pathways\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-18T12:11:32.362Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s3xw35xczs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s3xw35xczs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Longlong","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gztnvfbsr6","title":"The impact of skin bacteria on Zika virus infections","description":"This dataset comprises data regarding studies we performed to unravel the effect of skin bacteria on the infection and disease outcome of Zika virus in mice. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-07-11T17:07:26.373Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gztnvfbsr6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gztnvfbsr6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sam","last_name":"Verwimp","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bacteria","Skin","Zika Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9zggbwf2wd","title":"Discovery of Drug Candidates that Inhibit and Repress Zika Virus Infection in the Fetal and Adult Brain","description":"Zika virus (ZIKV) infects fetal and adult human brain and is associated with serious neurological complications.\nTo date, no therapeutic treatment is available to treat ZIKV-infected patients. We performed a high-content chemical screen using human pluripotent stem cell-derived cortical neural progenitor cells (hNPCs) and found that hippeastrine hydrobromide (HH) and amodiaquine dihydrochloride dihydrate (AQ) can inhibit ZIKV infection in hNPCs. Further validation showed that HH also rescues ZIKV-induced growth and differentiation defects in\nhNPCs and human fetal-like forebrain organoids. Finally, HH and AQ inhibit ZIKV infection in adult mouse brain in vivo. Strikingly, HH suppresses viral propagation when administered to adult mice with active ZIKV infection, highlighting its therapeutic potential. Our approach highlights the power of stem cell-based screens and validation in human forebrain organoids and mouse models in identifying drug candidates for treating ZIKV infection and related neurological complications in fetal and adult patients.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-10-25T21:33:26.044Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9zggbwf2wd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9zggbwf2wd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Shuibing","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ting","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lei","last_name":"Tan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gustav","last_name":"Cederquist","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Drug Screening","Zika Virus"],"journal_issn":["19345909"]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bgxfwsg3sb","title":"A comprehensive analysis and resource to use CRISPR-Cas13 for broad-spectrum targeting of RNA viruses. Lin et al.","description":"The COVID-19 pandemic caused by SARS-CoV-2 and variants has led to significant mortality. We recently reported that an RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas13 system, termed prophylactic antiviral CRISPR in human (PAC-MAN), offered an antiviral strategy against SARS-CoV-2 and influenza A virus. Here, we expand in silico analysis to use PAC-MAN to target a broad spectrum of human- or livestock-infectious RNA viruses with high specificity, coverage, and predicted efficiency. Our analysis reveals that a minimal set of 14 crRNAs is able to target >90% of human-infectious viruses across 10 RNA virus families. We predict that a set of 5 experimentally validated crRNAs can target new SARS-CoV-2 variant sequences with 0 mismatches. We also build an online resource (crispr-pacman.stanford.edu) to support community use of CRISPR-Cas13 for broad-spectrum RNA virus targeting. Our work provides a new bioinformatic resource for using CRISPR-Cas13 to target diverse RNA viruses in order to facilitate development of CRISPR-based antivirals. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-22T16:33:31.569Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bgxfwsg3sb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bgxfwsg3sb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xueqiu","last_name":"Lin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yanxia","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Augustine","last_name":"Chemparathy","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tara","last_name":"Pande","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marie","last_name":"La Russa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stanley","last_name":"Qi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bioengineering","Antiviral","Computational Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4mb5kjywkj","title":"Western Blot raw data of the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\"","description":"The raw images of WB is for the reviewers' request when reviewing the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-04T00:26:32.621Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4mb5kjywkj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4mb5kjywkj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mingang","last_name":"Hao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":86013,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":116,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":86013,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":85774,"restricted":49,"embargoed":190},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation 